GMAN 0.6.0
==========

GMAN began in 1999, was shelved in 2002, and resumed development in 2026.

What renders
------------

- All seven RenderMan quadrics: Sphere, Cone, Cylinder, Hyperboloid,
  Paraboloid, Torus, Disk.
- Convex Polygon.
- Three C++ surface shaders: matte, plastic, metal.
- Three light types: ambientlight, distantlight, pointlight.
- TIFF, PNG, JPEG and PNM output.

The RIB front end parses a subset of the RISpec 3.2 request set: the
quadrics and Polygon above, RIB-driven camera and transform setup, lights
and surface shaders, array and inline-declared parameters, facevarying
parameters, gzip'd RIB, ReadArchive with cycle detection, and graceful
recovery from any request GMAN does not recognize (warn once, skip, keep
parsing). It does not cover the full request set.

What does not render
---------------------

- Texturing.
- Antialiasing (one sample per pixel).
- REYES and ray tracing (renderer plugins present, non-functional, built
  OFF by default).
- Subdivision surfaces.
- The RenderMan shading-language compiler; shaders are C++ plugins.
- Patch, PatchMesh, NuPatch, curves and points: parse but do not
  rasterize.

`libgman/gmanribtokenize.cpp`'s `parseKeyword` is ground truth for which
requests the tokenizer recognizes at all; `libgman/gmanribparse.cpp`'s
request switch is ground truth for which of those reach a renderer call.
See AGENTS.md's "RIB authoring" section, "What the RIB front end actually
supports," for how to read them.
